<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Turpan Khanate</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Turpan Khanate was one of the successor states of the eastern branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Empire</a>, centred on the oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>. The eastern khanate split around 1462 into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent</a> and Turpan khanates. The Chagataid dynastic line survived in these and later successors for several centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turpan-khanate.jpg" alt="Turpan Khanate" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Qiushufang · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1517_Yarkent_and_Turpan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The khanate, also known as Eastern Moghulistan, the Kingdom of Uyghurstan or the Turfan Khanate, was a Sunni Muslim Turco-Mongol state ruled by descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khan">Chagatai Khan</a>. It was the eastern division of Moghulistan, itself an eastern offshoot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>. Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-alaq">Ahmad Alaq</a>, broke away from his brother Mahmud and ruled the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a>, at what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>. Under Ahmad Alaq and his eldest son Mansur, Turpan became more Muslim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-alaq">Ahmad Alaq</a> made peace with Ming China, with which his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunus-khan">Yunus Khan</a> had contested the Kara Del kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Hami</a>, and exchanged envoys with it. In the early 1500s he was defeated and killed in a battle against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-bukhara">Khanate of Bukhara</a>. His successor Mansur took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksu">Aksu</a>, drove out his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-said-khan">Sultan Said Khan</a>, and in 1513 conquered the Kara Del kingdom; the Buddhists of the Hami area then migrated into Ming territory. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-muhammad-haidar-dughlat">Mirza Muhammad Haidar Dughlat</a> called Mansur's war with the Ming over Hami a holy war.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-said-khan">Sultan Said Khan</a>, sheltered by his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a> and declared himself khan in 1514. Blocked in the steppe by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a>, he took the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkand">Yarkand</a>, and his state became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a>. The brothers were later reconciled, and the two khanates existed side by side. From the 16th century leaders of the Khojas gained strong influence, replacing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dughlat">Dughlat</a> faction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After Mansur's death the khanate declined rapidly under Shah Khan. In 1570 an army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a> led by Abduraim Sultan, governor of Khotan and brother of Abdul Karim Khan, invaded and took the ruler Muhammad Khan ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansur-khan">Mansur Khan</a> prisoner, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> came under the control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkand">Yarkand</a>. The last that was heard of the khanate were embassies sent from Turpan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1647 and 1657, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> regarded as coming from a genuine Chagatayid.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Turpan Khanate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
